HOW'D YOU AND MOM MEET, DADDY?

LAS VEGAS, NV (DPI) -- After conducting audience feedback surveys, the
FOX television network announced today that it is going to add a bit of
spark to its next installment of "Who Wants to Marry a
Multimillionaire."  The show, in which 50 contestants sell themselves in
a beauty-pageant-like setting for the chance to wed a multi-millionaire
they have never met, was deemed "too fantasy like," "too routine," and
"nothing like the real life experience of prostitution" by those
polled.  "The audience connected with the overall concept and liked what
they saw," explained VP of Special Programming Jeb Everton, responsible
for such FOX specials as "Blind Date Sex" and "Is She Too Young or
Not?", "but for them it just didn't have the drama the common man
associates with paid-for sex."

To bring the experience more in line with that drama, FOX is planning to
throw in an element of suspense.  "Although the contestants will still
look like your all-American girls," Everton reports, "we are going to
add a few transvestites, crack addicts, thieves, and women with sexually
transmittable diseases."  These changes, Everton assures, along with the
addition of a "Honeymoon cam," are sure to garner the show a spot in the
regular fall lineup."  In the typical creatively bankrupt fashion,
networks hedged their bets by rushing several similar shows into
pre-production to cover all demographics.  Soon to be on your fall
schedule:  "Who Wants to Marry a Guy with a Fully Furnished Double-Wide
Trailer," "Who Wants to Marry an Xtreme Dude Who Has a Case of Mountain
Dew and Tickets to See Blink-182," and "I've Got 40 Bucks, Be My Wife
For Ten Minutes in the Back of My Pinto."

Reported by Davejames

