GREED IS GOOD?  NOT ON TELEVISION
By Rick Steelhammer (February 20, 2000)

The sudden success of the Regis Philbin game show "Who Wants to be a
Millionaire?" has spawned a set of clones that have truly put the "ick"
in mimicking.

"Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?" begat "Greed," which mutated into
"Winning Lines," which in turn evolved into the latest and most
outrageous spin-off, last week's "Who Wants to Marry a
Multimillionaire?"

For those of you who were watching public TV or browsing through
"Ulysses" last Tuesday, "Who Wants to Marry a Multimillionaire" featured
the shameless efforts of 50 nubile young gold-diggers to convince a
middle-aged real estate magnate they were bride material.  All agreed
that if picked by the tycoon, they would wed him on the spot, sight
unseen.

The show dominated the ratings during a sweeps period, prompting Fox,
the network that gave us "When Good Pets Go Bad," to rerun a shortened
version of the show later this week.

Although you can smell it from here, the bottom of the barrel is not yet
in sight, given the success of the new greed-driven genre.

Here are some likely spin-offs:

'Who Wants to Be My Organ Donor?'

You gotta have heart -- or liver, or kidney -- to spare in order to cash
in on this new game show, hosted by "ER's" Anthony Edwards.  Deep-pocket
transplant patients unwilling to wait their turn on donor lists wage a
bidding war to acquire healthy organs STAT from cash-strapped members of
the studio audience.

'Get Scrooged'

Fat-cat contestants vie for the bragging rights that come with the
America's Tightest Multi-Millionaire designation -- earned by listening
to, but cynically rejecting, hard-luck-story appeals for cash from
contestants in the studio audience.

'Ka-Ching For a Day'

The first contestant who correctly answers the question, "What's your
middle name?" earns the equivalent of a day's worth of Bill Gates'
pre-tax income.  Laughs come quickly when the winner is forced to spend
his or her new cash windfall in one day -- usually on tacky "new money"
purchases like inept politicians, pro football teams, condos in Branson,
Mo., or full-size pickups will full tanks of high test.

'America's Money-est Stick-Up Videos'

Ooh and aah over surveillance camera footage of the nation's biggest
armed robberies.  Show you the money, you say?  Get the scoop as you
watch stick-up artists scoop out the cash drawers of some of the
nation's more prosperous banks, Lexus dealerships and presidential
candidate's "soft money" funds.

