KISSING A MILLIONAIRE
By Art Buchwald (March 2, 2000)

Television does have an effect on our young people.  The other evening I
sat around with three teens, and we got to discussing the defunct show
"Who Wants to Marry a Multimillionaire?"  Their reaction was different
from mine.

Theresa commented, "My mother said I could never marry a millionaire
because no rich person would want me."

Lola said, "That's unfair.  I read where many millionaires marry women
who are inferior to them."

Deidre said, "I don't think it's up to the millionaire.  It is up to the
poverty-stricken woman who is looking for her 15 minutes of fame on
television."

Theresa said, "Some millionaires are slobs.  They think just because
they have Arabian polo ponies that they are better than everybody else.
If I went on TV looking for a man I would want one who was
understanding, kind and would let me fly first class on our honeymoon."

I said, "The problem is that on TV you don't have enough time to
differentiate the good millionaires from the bad ones.  How does anyone
know?"

Lola said, "That's what TV producers are for.  They have to check out
the haves against the have-nots.  The first time they tried it they got
in trouble because the lady who got married was furious and wanted an
annulment."

"Why?" Deidre asked.

"She said her husband was a lousy kisser."

"The producers should have known that," Theresa said.

Then Deidre asked, "Are most millionaires lousy kissers?"

"They are in this respect.  They think the public should kiss them
instead of them kissing the public."

"Where did they learn that?" Deidre asked.

I said, "At Harvard."

Theresa asked, "Why would anyone want to marry a millionaire on
television?"

Lola said, "I could use the fame.  Also I might get lucky and marry
someone like Prince William.  At the same time if some nerd has made his
dough in the computer business, then count me out."

Theresa said, "I could handle anyone.  Once the show is over I can break
him into a hundred pieces."

Deidre said, "It would be a great way of getting into show business.  He
just better keep his hands off me during the commercials."

